If it's not true, you just
throw it out and move out

00:08:29.544 --> 00:08:31.398
because as you go there, we're
here because we're admitting

00:08:31.398 --> 00:08:33.577
we need something better in our lives.

00:08:33.577 --> 00:08:34.623
We're here because we're admitting

00:08:34.623 --> 00:08:35.793
we're a little bit broken.

00:08:35.793 --> 00:08:37.315
There's something we
need to learn we haven't.

00:08:37.315 --> 00:08:38.510
So as you do it,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:40.826
you want to take in all
the criticism that comes in

00:08:40.826 --> 00:08:42.972
and work on what you need to work on,

00:08:42.972 --> 00:08:44.678
so don't take it personally.

00:08:44.678 --> 00:08:46.260
- How do you think of yourself?

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:47.667
How do you describe yourself?

00:08:47.667 --> 00:08:50.408
And I think about this
because words matter,

00:08:50.408 --> 00:08:53.893
and I know that we're all
becoming a bunch of hyphens,

00:08:53.893 --> 00:08:55.153
like I consider myself a photographer,

00:08:55.153 --> 00:08:56.438
director, entrepreneur.

00:08:56.438 --> 00:08:57.512
I'm getting tired of it.

00:08:57.512 --> 00:08:58.916
How do you describe yourself?

00:08:58.916 --> 00:09:00.282
- I mean if I had to say like one thing

00:09:00.282 --> 00:09:01.633
maybe it would be a storyteller.

00:09:01.633 --> 00:09:03.437
I just like to tell
really great, compelling

00:09:03.437 --> 00:09:06.474
stories that if it's a
book, you can't put it down.

00:09:06.474 --> 00:09:10.205
If it's a movie or a TV
show, you can't turn it off.

00:09:10.205 --> 00:09:12.106
So to me, it's the art of storytelling,

00:09:12.106 --> 00:09:13.578
which I think is probably
one of the oldest

00:09:13.578 --> 00:09:15.506
human art forms there is.

00:09:15.506 --> 00:09:17.602
- Yeah, like caveman
times, they're like sitting

00:09:17.602 --> 00:09:19.323
around the fire, even like that's what

00:09:19.323 --> 00:09:20.298
cave paintings are, right?

00:09:20.298 --> 00:09:21.965
They're scratching out a story.

00:09:21.965 --> 00:09:23.013
- I always encourage people.

00:09:23.013 --> 00:09:24.917
This is probably true for
people listening right now

00:09:24.917 --> 00:09:26.514
that people love to read nonfiction.

00:09:26.514 --> 00:09:28.623
They want to get a book that's
going to tell them what to do

00:09:28.623 --> 00:09:31.147
and a bulleted list of what
the should or are supposed

00:09:31.147 --> 00:09:33.391
to do, but I really believe that the

00:09:33.391 --> 00:09:35.228
mind learns through metaphor.

00:09:35.228 --> 00:09:36.828
Right, that storytelling is to learn

00:09:36.828 --> 00:09:38.666
how you learn the roles of your tribe

00:09:38.666 --> 00:09:40.730
or your culture or the world or

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.471
about being a human being,
you learn through metaphor,

00:09:43.471 --> 00:09:46.031
and I really think we
undervalue storytelling

00:09:46.031 --> 00:09:47.790
this like quick fix culture.

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:48.639
- Oh

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:52.028
- When Tim said that I'm,
you know, I'm very methodical

00:09:52.028 --> 00:09:54.076
about time management
because it's so hard to do.

00:09:54.076 --> 00:09:56.416
So the first things, I'll
give you a couple of important

00:09:56.416 --> 00:09:57.780
life changing tips for anyone trying

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.161
to do something creative on the computer.

00:09:59.161 --> 00:10:00.994
So number one thing is

00:10:03.365 --> 00:10:04.204
no e-mail in the morning.

00:10:04.204 --> 00:10:06.583
Like number one thing is
there's a program called Freedom

00:10:06.583 --> 00:10:08.571
that I downloaded, and it says.

00:10:08.571 --> 00:10:10.371
And I love the word freedom

00:10:10.371 --> 00:10:11.858
because actually what it is, it says,

00:10:11.858 --> 00:10:13.491
"How many minutes of freedom do you want?"

00:10:13.491 --> 00:10:14.578
You say whatever it is, 90 minutes,

00:10:14.578 --> 00:10:17.197
and then you cannot access
the internet for 90 minutes.

00:10:17.197 --> 00:10:18.503
There's no unlock code.

00:10:18.503 --> 00:10:19.552
People are gasping.

00:10:19.552 --> 00:10:20.385
You're all going to use it, yeah.

00:10:20.385 --> 00:10:23.601
There's no unlock 'cause
the first thing I do is

00:10:23.601 --> 00:10:25.666
phone, I give to someone else

00:10:25.666 --> 00:10:26.839
or put it in a drawer somewhere.

00:10:26.839 --> 00:10:28.495
It's best if I give it to someone else.

00:10:28.495 --> 00:10:31.075
Internet goes off, and
I commit to that amount,

00:10:31.075 --> 00:10:34.067
a certain amount of time, so

00:10:34.067 --> 00:10:35.940
my thing is that it's compartmentalizing.

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.208
That if you're going to check your e-mail,

00:10:37.208 --> 00:10:39.304
check it from four to five.

00:10:39.304 --> 00:10:40.858
They have a, there's a,
someone wrote a book,

00:10:40.858 --> 00:10:41.780
which I never read that's called,

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.481
"Never Check Your E-mail in the Morning."

00:10:43.481 --> 00:10:44.769
You don't, I don't know if
you're going to read it or not,

00:10:44.769 --> 00:10:47.071
but just know the title,
and that's really great.

00:10:47.071 --> 00:10:49.523
It's great, but the thing is with time,

00:10:49.523 --> 00:10:51.422
it's to become proactive about time

00:10:51.422 --> 00:10:53.897
instead of reactive because
I know so many people

00:10:53.897 --> 00:10:56.690
who can't do anything because
you're getting a message

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:58.299
on Facebook, and then you're getting a,

00:10:58.299 --> 00:11:00.347
an e-mail, and then you're
just reacting to that

00:11:00.347 --> 00:11:01.755
because it feels like you're
getting something done.

00:11:01.755 --> 00:11:03.338
At the end of the day,
you got nothing done,

00:11:03.338 --> 00:11:04.616
so I, what my thing is I'm really,

00:11:04.616 --> 00:11:06.613
and everyone knows that
I'm proactive about that

00:11:06.613 --> 00:11:08.384
to the degree that,

00:11:08.384 --> 00:11:09.494
and by the way I'd have Facebook

00:11:09.494 --> 00:11:10.996
and Twitter blocked on my computer.

00:11:10.996 --> 00:11:13.491
What I did is I downloaded Intego Content,

00:11:13.491 --> 00:11:17.236
I think it's a content
barrier, whatever it is.

00:11:17.236 --> 00:11:18.781
- [Interviewer] The rest
of time can be used.

00:11:18.781 --> 00:11:19.952
- Net Nanny.

00:11:19.952 --> 00:11:21.779
- Net Nanny can be used
to block specific sites.

00:11:21.779 --> 00:11:24.336
And I had someone else put in
a password for those sites,

00:11:24.336 --> 00:11:25.480
so I don't even know the passwords,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.933
so I couldn't get into Twitter
or Facebook if I tried.

00:11:28.933 --> 00:11:30.635
So finding those things that are those

00:11:30.635 --> 00:11:32.355
and putting walls between you and them

00:11:32.355 --> 00:11:33.452
or limiting them in certain ways.

00:11:33.452 --> 00:11:35.072
There's one other thing
I was going to say was.

00:11:35.072 --> 00:11:38.409
Oh, which is, friends
can be a pain in the ass

00:11:38.409 --> 00:11:39.813
if you're trying to be creative,

00:11:39.813 --> 00:11:40.899
and if you have more than seven friends,

00:11:40.899 --> 00:11:42.082
and everyone wants to
see you one day a week,

00:11:42.082 --> 00:11:43.059
what do you do?

00:11:43.059 --> 00:11:44.375
So another thing I want to say is

00:11:44.375 --> 00:11:46.409
I do a Wednesday night dinner party,

00:11:46.409 --> 00:11:47.872
so if my friends want to see me, I'm like,

00:11:47.872 --> 00:11:48.812
"Great, Wednesday."

00:11:48.812 --> 00:11:50.028
If someone wants a meeting
who I'm not sure about,

00:11:50.028 --> 00:11:52.760
Wednesday night, so
Wednesday night, we all go to

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:55.834
a restaurant and basically,
whoever I want to see

00:11:55.834 --> 00:11:57.564
or catch up on, I'll see them there,

00:11:57.564 --> 00:11:59.027
and then maybe afterward I'll break off

00:11:59.027 --> 00:12:00.467
with my closer friends, but that way,

00:12:00.467 --> 00:12:02.402
I can see everyone, people
who I want to meet with,

00:12:02.402 --> 00:12:03.317
but I don't really want to take the time

00:12:03.317 --> 00:12:05.944
for a lunch or meeting one-on-one.

00:12:05.944 --> 00:12:06.816
If I like them I talk to them a bunch.

00:12:06.816 --> 00:12:08.607
If I don't, I put them far away.

00:12:08.607 --> 00:12:09.743
Humility is the most important thing

00:12:09.743 --> 00:12:11.505
that you can have to work for change.

00:12:11.505 --> 00:12:12.968
If you really want to have a big change.

00:12:12.968 --> 00:12:14.882
A lot of people will come
just to hear what they

00:12:14.882 --> 00:12:16.616
already want to hear like codify this

00:12:16.616 --> 00:12:18.479
into five principles I
can then apply to my life.

00:12:18.479 --> 00:12:20.211
You know you get your codify is the word,

00:12:20.211 --> 00:12:23.127
and I'll codify, I'll codify
some self-awareness for you.

00:12:23.127 --> 00:12:25.210
But humility to recognize

00:12:26.351 --> 00:12:28.935
you know, what got you
here won't get you there

00:12:28.935 --> 00:12:30.150
as the saying goes, right?

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.891
And to say at some point,

00:12:32.891 --> 00:12:34.441
if you really want to change
and you're really stuck,

00:12:34.441 --> 00:12:37.025
"I know nothing, I'm
letting go of everything,

00:12:37.025 --> 00:12:39.829
"and I'm sort of going to relearn

00:12:39.829 --> 00:12:41.950
"as with like a childlike mind."

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.315
We treat ourselves horribly,

00:12:44.315 --> 00:12:48.153
we talk to ourselves in ways
that we would never, ever

00:12:48.153 --> 00:12:50.530
allow a stranger to talk to us.

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.566
We take your self,

00:12:52.566 --> 00:12:54.712
if someone in here walked
up to you and said,

00:12:54.712 --> 00:12:56.501
"You're not interesting,"
"You've got nothing good to say,"

00:12:56.501 --> 00:12:59.302
"You're not enough,"
"You're not attractive,"

00:12:59.302 --> 00:13:01.790
you'd be like, "you
(bleep), mother (bleep)."

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:03.241
(laughter)

00:13:03.241 --> 00:13:04.074
Right?

00:13:04.074 --> 00:13:05.837
(laughter)

00:13:05.837 --> 00:13:10.238
So, why do we allow ourselves
to talk to ourself that way?

00:13:10.238 --> 00:13:13.056
Right, so there's sort of a
message that I will give you

00:13:13.056 --> 00:13:14.982
guys today as I'm trying
to help you get over

00:13:14.982 --> 00:13:18.005
approaching anxiety, is start
treating yourself the way

00:13:18.005 --> 00:13:19.820
you want that person you're approaching

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.478
to be treating you, right?

00:13:21.478 --> 00:13:23.767
If she says that she,
she's not going to say

00:13:23.767 --> 00:13:27.574
anything half as mean as the
(shit) you're telling yourself.

00:13:27.574 --> 00:13:30.176
So you have nothing to
fear anymore, right?

00:13:30.176 --> 00:13:32.590
'Cause you all have given
yourself the worst of it.

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:35.261
If I could give you like one
thing this whole weekend,

00:13:35.261 --> 00:13:37.757
it'd be to stop talking
to yourself this way.

00:13:37.757 --> 00:13:40.879
So every version has this
course while I aim action.

00:13:40.879 --> 00:13:42.415
You may feel like you're getting nothing

00:13:42.415 --> 00:13:44.341
but rejection, right, but
where is that coming from?

00:13:44.341 --> 00:13:47.521
Like you were saying earlier,
if you're giving someone

00:13:47.521 --> 00:13:49.875
more worth and value,
higher status than yourself,

00:13:49.875 --> 00:13:52.996
you're loving them more
what is that saying?

00:13:52.996 --> 00:13:55.727
That's shame, shame about who you are,

00:13:55.727 --> 00:13:58.018
shame that you're not worthy
to talk to this person.

00:13:58.018 --> 00:14:00.763
Shame that this person somehow
still has some power over you

00:14:00.763 --> 00:14:03.030
or is going to hurt you,
and that you're not enough.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:03.863
That you're not good enough.

00:14:03.863 --> 00:14:04.859
That you're not attractive enough.

00:14:04.859 --> 00:14:05.883
That you're not

00:14:05.883 --> 00:14:06.716
rich enough.

00:14:06.716 --> 00:14:07.551
That you're not socially
comfortable enough

00:14:07.551 --> 00:14:09.133
or too awkward, whatever it is.

00:14:09.133 --> 00:14:10.540
That's shame about who you are.

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.353
And what happens, right, your shame

00:14:12.353 --> 00:14:14.853
when you walk in turns to joy.

00:14:16.207 --> 00:14:18.485
So the whole idea is, if
you're going to approach

00:14:18.485 --> 00:14:22.485
from a place of joy instead
of a place of shame,

00:14:23.523 --> 00:14:26.022
you will have no approaching anxiety.

00:14:26.022 --> 00:14:27.777
And that's what we call,
what I call and they call

00:14:27.777 --> 00:14:31.615
having an A game day is being in state.

00:14:31.615 --> 00:14:34.956
You're being in state, which
is just really having joy.

00:14:34.956 --> 00:14:36.467
So they walk in and you walk up,

00:14:36.467 --> 00:14:38.704
and you're like, "Okay,
all right there are all

00:14:38.704 --> 00:14:39.885
"these people, and they're
going to approve of me,

00:14:39.885 --> 00:14:41.104
"and if they approve of me, I'm going

00:14:41.104 --> 00:14:42.483
"to feel good about myself,
and if they don't approve

00:14:42.483 --> 00:14:44.617
"of me, I'm going to feel
(bleep) about myself,

00:14:44.617 --> 00:14:45.753
"and I'm going to go out
and put my self-esteem

00:14:45.753 --> 00:14:48.251
"in the hands of every
stranger I talk to."

00:14:48.251 --> 00:14:50.071
You're just setting
yourself up for trouble.

00:14:50.071 --> 00:14:51.092
You know, you can push past it

00:14:51.092 --> 00:14:53.345
using a lot of this
full mentality of love,

00:14:53.345 --> 00:14:56.408
but you're starting off with
a giant, giant handicap.

00:14:56.408 --> 00:14:58.491
Don't hold anything back.

00:14:59.437 --> 00:15:02.382
When someone asked Heidi
earlier what had really worked

00:15:02.382 --> 00:15:03.745
for her, she was like,
I think you said like,

00:15:03.745 --> 00:15:05.795
"Sharing my feelings."

00:15:05.795 --> 00:15:06.820
Is that what you said?

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:08.561
And I find that so many people
when they're going to write,

00:15:08.561 --> 00:15:11.379
they just want to show
like a glorified side

00:15:11.379 --> 00:15:13.511
of themselves that everyone can relate to.

00:15:13.511 --> 00:15:16.327
There's a quote from Carl Rogers,

00:15:16.327 --> 00:15:20.860
the psychologist, who says,
"The personal is the universal."

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.541
I know so many people who
think they're going to appeal

00:15:22.541 --> 00:15:25.004
to everyone by being
generic and universal,

00:15:25.004 --> 00:15:26.747
and you appeal to nobody,

00:15:26.747 --> 00:15:28.491
but if you talk from
your heart, and you say

00:15:28.491 --> 00:15:30.076
the most personal stuff,
the stuff you're scared.

00:15:30.076 --> 00:15:31.953
By the way, the only
book, reason my books,

00:15:31.953 --> 00:15:34.210
I think are successful is
'cause I say the (bleep)

00:15:34.210 --> 00:15:36.480
that I'm too scared to tell
my friends or tell people.

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.192
Somehow I can write it,
and when you write it,

00:15:38.192 --> 00:15:40.595
people read it, "Oh
yeah, I relate to that.

00:15:40.595 --> 00:15:42.375
"No one's said that before,
but that's exactly what I'm,

00:15:42.375 --> 00:15:43.970
"what I'm going through."

00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:45.553
So don't hold back.

00:15:47.275 --> 00:15:48.108
There's a difference between

00:15:48.108 --> 00:15:51.152
just vomiting emotion all over the page.

00:15:51.152 --> 00:15:52.943
It still has to be,
follow the number one rule

00:15:52.943 --> 00:15:55.663
of being interesting, right,
but the stuff you're too scared

00:15:55.663 --> 00:15:57.261
to tell everyone, that's the stuff

00:15:57.261 --> 00:15:59.797
you should be writing about and sharing.

00:15:59.797 --> 00:16:01.260
And that thought that you think maybe

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:03.486
is going to get you unliked,
and it's a scary time

00:16:03.486 --> 00:16:06.086
right now in the culture because

00:16:06.086 --> 00:16:08.598
everything, we get instant
feedback on everything,

00:16:08.598 --> 00:16:10.232
right, everything we write,
we get instant feedback.

00:16:10.232 --> 00:16:12.318
We know everybody's opinion, which

00:16:12.318 --> 00:16:14.802
if you don't have a rock
solid sense of who you are

00:16:14.802 --> 00:16:17.971
and what you're doing
can lead to just trying

00:16:17.971 --> 00:16:22.004
to conform and be liked,
and I promise you.

00:16:22.004 --> 00:16:24.242
I wrote a book called "Everyone
Loves You When You're Dead,"

00:16:24.242 --> 00:16:26.290
and the reason, it was
interviews with celebrities

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.189
because when you're dead,
you're no longer competition.

00:16:28.189 --> 00:16:30.689
As long as you're here, right?

00:16:31.777 --> 00:16:34.637
If no one doesn't like what you're doing,

00:16:34.637 --> 00:16:36.219
then you're probably not
doing anything interesting.

00:16:36.219 --> 00:16:37.211
Right?

00:16:37.211 --> 00:16:39.928
If everyone, if you're only
getting positive feedback,

00:16:39.928 --> 00:16:41.014
then you're probably not doing

00:16:41.014 --> 00:16:42.609
anything new or interesting, right?

00:16:42.609 --> 00:16:45.276
So let some negativity be a sign

00:16:46.178 --> 00:16:47.855
that "Okay, good, I'm actually saying

00:16:47.855 --> 00:16:50.091
"something new and something meaningful."

00:16:50.091 --> 00:16:52.682
(swoosh)

00:16:52.682 --> 00:16:54.361
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:54.361 --> 00:16:57.216
I made this video because
James Esler asked me to.

00:16:57.216 --> 00:16:59.070
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.118
to profile next leave it
in the comments below,

00:17:01.118 --> 00:17:02.167
and I'll see what I can do.

00:17:02.167 --> 00:17:03.518
I'd also love to know what did Neil say

00:17:03.518 --> 00:17:05.105
that had the biggest impact on you.

00:17:05.105 --> 00:17:07.126
What lesson did you take from this video

00:17:07.126 --> 00:17:08.467
that you're going to immediately

00:17:08.467 --> 00:17:10.773
apply to your life or business?

00:17:10.773 --> 00:17:11.734
Leave it in the comments.

00:17:11.734 --> 00:17:13.382
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:17:13.382 --> 00:17:15.354
Finally, want to give a quick
shoutout to Phanta Media.

00:17:15.354 --> 00:17:18.000
Thank you guys so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:21.023
"Your One Word" and posting
that great picture to Instagram.

00:17:21.023 --> 00:17:22.817
I really, really, really appreciate it.

00:17:22.817 --> 00:17:24.217
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:17:24.217 --> 00:17:25.645
I believe in you.

00:17:25.645 --> 00:17:27.815
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:17:27.815 --> 00:17:30.093
and whatever your one word is.

00:17:30.093 --> 00:17:32.350
Much love. I'll see you soon.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.433
(swoosh)

00:17:38.177 --> 00:17:41.857
- Number one rule in writing is this.

00:17:41.857 --> 00:17:43.589
Nobody cares.

00:17:43.589 --> 00:17:45.221
Nobody cares about you.

00:17:45.221 --> 00:17:46.589
They don't care about your thoughts.

00:17:46.589 --> 00:17:48.271
They don't care about your ideas.

00:17:48.271 --> 00:17:49.598
They don't care about
what you have to say,

00:17:49.598 --> 00:17:51.134
what you did yesterday, or anything.

00:17:51.134 --> 00:17:54.510
They do not give a (bleep).

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:57.240
And from there can you make them care?

00:17:57.240 --> 00:18:00.446
Everything I write, I
assume that nobody cares,

00:18:00.446 --> 00:18:02.314
that they have no interest, and if I start

00:18:02.314 --> 00:18:04.829
from that perspective,
and if I can draw you in

00:18:04.829 --> 00:18:07.615
through the writing, that's
where the story begins.

00:18:07.615 --> 00:18:10.102
So just assume, start by
assuming nobody cares,

00:18:10.102 --> 00:18:14.269
and how can you draw them in
to your story or your point.

00:18:15.241 --> 00:18:17.577
Number two then, one way to draw them in

00:18:17.577 --> 00:18:19.380
is something called the open loops.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.951
Who here knows what an open loop is?

00:18:21.951 --> 00:18:25.121
Cool, has anyone seen the
new Doctor Strange movie?

00:18:25.121 --> 00:18:26.350
Anyone seen that?

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.886
Cool, Kyle you saw it, so is it good?

00:18:28.886 --> 00:18:29.973
Okay by the way, that's an open loop.

00:18:29.973 --> 00:18:32.129
An open loop was I asked
you what an open loop was

00:18:32.129 --> 00:18:33.484
and then rather than explaining it,

00:18:33.484 --> 00:18:35.093
I went on to another subject and talked

00:18:35.093 --> 00:18:36.654
about Doctor Strange or something.

00:18:36.654 --> 00:18:39.355
So an open loop is, I
can start with something

00:18:39.355 --> 00:18:41.403
that captures your
attention and 98 percent

00:18:41.403 --> 00:18:42.681
of you didn't know what an open loop is.

00:18:42.681 --> 00:18:43.874
You're probably curious about it by now

00:18:43.874 --> 00:18:44.949
because I've been talking for two minutes

00:18:44.949 --> 00:18:46.802
and still haven't explained it yet, right?

00:18:46.802 --> 00:18:49.532
An open loop is that at the
top I tell you something

00:18:49.532 --> 00:18:51.082
that you're interested in and you want

00:18:51.082 --> 00:18:52.848
to know the answer to
and wait 'til the end.

00:18:52.848 --> 00:18:54.714
How many times have we just
stayed through a really

00:18:54.714 --> 00:18:56.519
(bleep) movie just 'cause you want to know

00:18:56.519 --> 00:18:58.338
what happens at the end, right?

00:18:58.338 --> 00:18:59.807
It can be the worst movie ever,

00:18:59.807 --> 00:19:01.834
but I want to know how
that happened, right?

00:19:01.834 --> 00:19:04.464
So an open, so one is to
use the idea of open loops,

00:19:04.464 --> 00:19:06.237
and it's true in relationships.

00:19:06.237 --> 00:19:07.540
You meet somebody you
have a lot in common with,

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:10.036
and you start telling all these stories.

00:19:10.036 --> 00:19:10.869
You want it to continue.

00:19:10.869 --> 00:19:12.528
So an open loop is a great
way to create intrigue

00:19:12.528 --> 00:19:15.990
because we're creatures who
love storytelling, right?

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.599
Early, early, storytelling
is part of our sort of

00:19:18.599 --> 00:19:22.766
our DNA, so creating open
loops at the top of a post or

00:19:23.708 --> 00:19:25.878
in a book is really powerful.

00:19:25.878 --> 00:19:27.474
So yeah the best, I'd say
the best advice for anyone

00:19:27.474 --> 00:19:29.717
who wants to write a book is literally,

00:19:29.717 --> 00:19:30.814
the best way to write a book is

00:19:30.814 --> 00:19:33.900
to have a looming, impending deadline

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:37.127
with hard, real world
consequences for missing it.

00:19:37.127 --> 00:19:38.501
- [Tim] Stakes, remember
stakes, guys? Honestly.

00:19:38.501 --> 00:19:39.735
- Yeah, did you know I put out stakes?

00:19:39.735 --> 00:19:40.968
- [Tim] Yeah, you need stakes.

00:19:40.968 --> 00:19:42.210
- Like stakes to be--

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:43.406
- [Tim] I talked to--

00:19:43.406 --> 00:19:45.245
- Day old diet or the S-T-A-K-E-S?

00:19:45.245 --> 00:19:47.331
- Oh yes, stakes. This is, yeah not like.

00:19:47.331 --> 00:19:51.470
Stakes like vampire stakes
but for consequences.

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:52.902
- And I think that.

00:19:52.902 --> 00:19:54.526
- That's something that a media
coach said to me recently,

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:57.310
and they were like when you
say stakes, and you have a book

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:58.283
called "The Four Hour Chef,"

00:19:58.283 --> 00:20:00.307
they're going to think
you're talking about steaks.

00:20:00.307 --> 00:20:02.122
Like (groan).

00:20:02.122 --> 00:20:04.305
- But I think if you can't
do it, you manufacturer it.

00:20:04.305 --> 00:20:05.866
You talk about it at stake.com,

00:20:05.866 --> 00:20:08.645
and things like that, but I
really know that literally,

00:20:08.645 --> 00:20:11.264
you know Tim's always,
Tim would be calling,

00:20:11.264 --> 00:20:13.946
and I would be calling Tim
saying, "I got to get this in.

00:20:13.946 --> 00:20:15.263
"I got like three months.

00:20:15.263 --> 00:20:16.959
"I can't do," but literally,
if we didn't have those

00:20:16.959 --> 00:20:18.119
deadlines you wouldn't have
written three books in this

00:20:18.119 --> 00:20:18.952
amount of time.
- [Interviewer] It would never

00:20:18.952 --> 00:20:20.451
have happened. It would
never have happened.

00:20:20.451 --> 00:20:21.865
- Yeah, so you have to,

00:20:21.865 --> 00:20:24.080
so I'll do that if a friend
of mine wants to write a book,

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.795
and they want me to proofread
it or take a look at it,

00:20:25.795 --> 00:20:27.520
I'll say, "I'll take a look
at it, but what date are you

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:28.692
"going to get it done by 'cause I'm going

00:20:28.692 --> 00:20:30.594
to hold you to it," and
they'll say, "January."

00:20:30.594 --> 00:20:32.781
I say, "If it's not done
by the end of March,

00:20:32.781 --> 00:20:34.225
"I'm not going to read your
book at all no matter what."

00:20:34.225 --> 00:20:37.334
So I'll give consequences
and deadlines to my friends,

00:20:37.334 --> 00:20:38.980
so it's a good way to write is to really

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.664
if you don't have a publisher or a deal

00:20:40.664 --> 00:20:42.513
or something like that, find some

00:20:42.513 --> 00:20:45.124
real consequences that's
externally imposed.

00:20:45.124 --> 00:20:48.549
You have to learn from your mistakes.

00:20:48.549 --> 00:20:52.023
No, I have this attitude that
no situation is impossible.

00:20:52.023 --> 00:20:53.586
There is nothing impossible.

00:20:53.586 --> 00:20:56.676
There're just contingencies
and obstacles to get around,

00:20:56.676 --> 00:20:58.343
so if I went out and

00:21:00.769 --> 00:21:01.803
I'm just trying to think of obstacles.

00:21:01.803 --> 00:21:03.015
Let's just say you did an opener,

00:21:03.015 --> 00:21:04.412
and she'd heard it already.

00:21:04.412 --> 00:21:06.283
You did your line, and
she'd heard it already

00:21:06.283 --> 00:21:07.148
because your friend just approached

00:21:07.148 --> 00:21:08.571
her with it 10 minutes ago.

00:21:08.571 --> 00:21:09.781
Do you run screaming?

00:21:09.781 --> 00:21:10.765
Maybe that happens the first time.

00:21:10.765 --> 00:21:11.659
The second time you say,

00:21:11.659 --> 00:21:12.971
"If that happens again, what do I do?"

00:21:12.971 --> 00:21:14.483
You say, "Oh my god, you
must have met my friend.

00:21:14.483 --> 00:21:15.316
"That's really funny."

00:21:15.316 --> 00:21:18.190
"We were just talking about
that," and problem solved.

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:21.951
Whatever it may be, say you,
any problem has a solution,

00:21:21.951 --> 00:21:23.434
so I kind of have that attitude.

00:21:23.434 --> 00:21:25.800
You know, what if she's with eight guys?

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:26.633
What if

00:21:27.656 --> 00:21:29.117
she is

00:21:29.117 --> 00:21:30.183
on her way out the door?

00:21:30.183 --> 00:21:31.708
Any problem is going to have a solution.

00:21:31.708 --> 00:21:33.832
So you have to have that attitude, and

00:21:33.832 --> 00:21:35.655
if something goes wrong,

00:21:35.655 --> 00:21:37.094
and I'm saying this in a positive way,

00:21:37.094 --> 00:21:38.497
not a negative self-esteem way,

00:21:38.497 --> 00:21:41.141
but if something goes wrong,
it's always your fault.

00:21:41.141 --> 00:21:42.056
That's what I feel like.

00:21:42.056 --> 00:21:43.260
I'm not being hard on myself if something

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.909
goes wrong, it's never her fault.

00:21:44.909 --> 00:21:46.038
She's not a bitch.

00:21:46.038 --> 00:21:47.748
She is not mean.

00:21:47.748 --> 00:21:49.162
She is not uptight.

00:21:49.162 --> 00:21:51.575
I did something wrong to
make her uncomfortable.

00:21:51.575 --> 00:21:54.428
So for example with the openers,

00:21:54.428 --> 00:21:55.905
I would start walking up and saying,

00:21:55.905 --> 00:21:57.741
"Hey guys, I want to get
an opinion on something.

00:21:57.741 --> 00:21:58.926
"I got this question I'm trying to ask.

00:21:58.926 --> 00:22:00.155
"Can I start asking it?"

00:22:00.155 --> 00:22:02.035
But I noticed they
weren't really listening.

00:22:02.035 --> 00:22:02.868
They were tuning me out.

00:22:02.868 --> 00:22:04.779
They were kind of giving
me bad body language.

00:22:04.779 --> 00:22:06.291
So I realized what are they thinking?

00:22:06.291 --> 00:22:07.470
Why is that happening?

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:09.520
Well it's happening because
I'm standing around.

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:10.972
They don't know when I'm going to leave,

00:22:10.972 --> 00:22:12.613
so I added what's called
a time constraint.

00:22:12.613 --> 00:22:13.701
I'd walk in, I'd say, "Hey guys,

00:22:13.701 --> 00:22:15.469
"I want to get a quick
opinion on something.

00:22:15.469 --> 00:22:16.726
"By the way, I can only stay for a second

00:22:16.726 --> 00:22:18.736
"'cause I got to rejoin
my friends over there."

00:22:18.736 --> 00:22:20.481
You know, problem solved.

00:22:20.481 --> 00:22:23.953
So any time something went
wrong or was I coming.

00:22:23.953 --> 00:22:26.099
Maybe I would do everything,
and I felt like I was doing

00:22:26.099 --> 00:22:28.561
it perfectly, but people
weren't interested.

00:22:28.561 --> 00:22:30.328
Well I was doing it, doing
all the right things,

00:22:30.328 --> 00:22:32.609
but I was doing it in a very needy way.

00:22:32.609 --> 00:22:33.720
A lot of people will read the material,

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:35.743
and they'll say, "This doesn't work."

00:22:35.743 --> 00:22:37.450
Everything works.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:39.993
What I say is going to be
different than what Zan says

00:22:39.993 --> 00:22:41.118
and different than what someone else says.

00:22:41.118 --> 00:22:42.250
It all works.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:43.543
You just have to do it right.

00:22:43.543 --> 00:22:46.676
So for example, the opinion
openers we're talking about.

00:22:46.676 --> 00:22:48.836
The opinion opener is how
you start a conversation.

00:22:48.836 --> 00:22:50.502
If you go and you say, "Can I ask

00:22:50.502 --> 00:22:52.377
"you a question on something?"

00:22:52.377 --> 00:22:54.332
And they say, "No, we're talking.

00:22:54.332 --> 00:22:55.419
"Get lost."

00:22:55.419 --> 00:22:56.252
Well

00:22:57.183 --> 00:22:58.322
the opinion openers don't work.

00:22:58.322 --> 00:22:59.217
You just did it wrong.

00:22:59.217 --> 00:23:00.968
You walk and you say,
"Hey guys, I want to ask

00:23:00.968 --> 00:23:02.248
a quick question on something."

00:23:02.248 --> 00:23:03.828
Now you haven't asked
them a yes no question,

00:23:03.828 --> 00:23:06.599
and it's going to work, so
there's so many little subtleties.

00:23:06.599 --> 00:23:07.826
Did that make sense?

00:23:07.826 --> 00:23:08.659
Okay.

00:23:08.659 --> 00:23:09.492
So there's so many.

00:23:09.492 --> 00:23:10.351
I saw someone looking at me strangely.

00:23:10.351 --> 00:23:11.351
So there's so many little subtleties

00:23:11.351 --> 00:23:14.084
that you have to work in,
and it's always, always

00:23:14.084 --> 00:23:15.494
going to be

00:23:15.494 --> 00:23:16.748
your fault that it's not working.

00:23:16.748 --> 00:23:19.346
Not in a negative way, and
not in being hard on yourself.

00:23:19.346 --> 00:23:22.076
It's feedback so you can improve yourself.

00:23:22.076 --> 00:23:24.186
That whole idea that there's
no such thing as failure.

00:23:24.186 --> 00:23:25.612
There are only learning lessons.

00:23:25.612 --> 00:23:26.445
So that's what I did,

00:23:26.445 --> 00:23:27.589
but you know what, I'm a
guy who's hard on myself,

00:23:27.589 --> 00:23:29.981
but I'm hard on myself because
it makes me excel usually.

